---
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Why is my MX lookup empty?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Either no MX record exists at the name you checked, or the records were removed. Mail falls back to the A record, which usually fails delivery." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "Can MX point to a CNAME?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "It should not: RFC 2181 section 10.3 prohibits MX targets pointing at CNAMEs. Point MX at an A or AAAA hostname." } },
    {'@type': 'Question', 'name': "How do I test mail delivery after fixing MX?", "acceptedAnswer": { '@type': 'Answer', "text": "Send from an external account and read the delivery headers, or run an SMTP-level check that walks the MX priorities end to end." } }
  ],
};
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

---
<BaseLayout title="MX Record Not Working? How to Diagnose and Fix" description="MX troubleshooting: no mail arriving, mail to the wrong server, and partial delivery, diagnosed step by step with live tools."  
 breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["DNS Guide", "/dns/"], ["MX Not Working"]]}>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">MX Not Working</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">MX Record Not Working?</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
      "Email is broken" almost always means one of four things in the MX layer. Work this list in
      order; each step has a live check.
    </p>
    </div>
<nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#1-do-the-mx-records-exist-at-all">1. Do the MX records exist at all?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#2-are-they-the-right-records">2. Are they the RIGHT records?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#3-do-all-resolvers-see-the-same-thing">3. Do all resolvers see the same thing?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#4-is-the-mail-server-itself-accepting">4. Is the mail server itself accepting?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#quick-reference-bounce-error-to-meaning">Quick reference: bounce error to meaning</a></li>
        <li><a href="#related">Related</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="1-do-the-mx-records-exist-at-all">1. Do the MX records exist at all?</h2>
      <p>
        Run the <a href="/dns-record-lookup/">record lookup</a> with MX. NXDOMAIN on your apex means
        bigger problems; NOERROR with no answers means the domain exists but no MX: senders fall back
        to your A record, and if nothing listens there, mail bounces.
      </p>
      <h2 id="2-are-they-the-right-records">2. Are they the RIGHT records?</h2>
      <p>
        Compare the answer against your mail provider's published values, character for character.
        A typo'd hostname (<code>aspmx.l.googel.com</code>) produces silent, total failure. Verify the
        target hostnames themselves resolve: MX chains break when the target's A record disappears.
      </p>
      <h2 id="3-do-all-resolvers-see-the-same-thing">3. Do all resolvers see the same thing?</h2>
      <p>
        After any change, cached old values cause partial delivery: some senders hit the old server.
        The <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">propagation checker</a> shows which resolvers still
        serve the previous set; wait or fix the TTL mistake that caused the long cache.
      </p>
      <h2 id="4-is-the-mail-server-itself-accepting">4. Is the mail server itself accepting?</h2>
      <p>
        DNS correct but mail still bouncing means the problem is past DNS: the provider's outage, a
        full mailbox, or greylisting. Bounce messages quote the SMTP error; read it, it names the
        failing stage.
      </p>
      <h2 id="quick-reference-bounce-error-to-meaning">Quick reference: bounce error to meaning</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60">
        <table class="pdns-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Bounce text</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-accent">no MX records / host not found</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Missing or typo'd MX (steps 1-2)</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-accent">connection timed out</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Mail host unreachable: wrong target, firewall, or outage</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-accent">450 / try later</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Greylisting or temporary failure; retry, usually benign</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-accent">mailbox full / quota</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Recipient-side, not DNS at all</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      
<h2 id="related">Related</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="/mx-record/">MX records explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/email-not-working-after-dns-change/">Email stopped after a DNS change</a> and <a href="/how-to-add-mx-record/">how to add them</a></li>
        <li><a href="/mx-record/">MX records explained</a></li>
        <li><a href="/email-not-working-after-dns-change/">Email stopped after a DNS change</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why is my MX lookup empty?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Either no MX record exists at the name you checked, or the records were removed. Mail falls back to the A record, which usually fails delivery.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can MX point to a CNAME?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">It should not: RFC 2181 section 10.3 prohibits MX targets pointing at CNAMEs. Point MX at an A or AAAA hostname.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">How do I test mail delivery after fixing MX?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Send from an external account and read the delivery headers, or run an SMTP-level check that walks the MX priorities end to end.</p></details></div>
      </div>
</BaseLayout>
